# Trivy suppression list -- format: one CVE per line, optional comment.
#
# Phase 3 (v3.10.13-13) reduced this file to only the libssl1.1 backport
# suppressions.  The Phase 1 log4j filename-mismatch block and the Phase 2B
# tomcat-embed-core SHA1-mismatch block were both retired:
#
#   - log4j filenames are now normalised by the Phase 3 lib/ rename (e.g.
#     log4j-core-2.17.2.jar holds 2.17.2 bytes; airvision's Manifest
#     Class-Path is rewritten to match via uv-patcher).  Trivy's filename-
#     derived fingerprint and content-derived fingerprint agree -- no more
#     false-positive 2.1 reports.
#
#   - tomcat-embed-core-9.0.118.jar is now shipped pristine from Maven
#     Central.  The Tomcat 9 Bootstrap instance-method shim is applied by
#     uv-patcher at container start (in the writable layer), so the image
#     layer's JAR has the original 9.0.118 SHA1 and Trivy matches the
#     trivy-java-db entry cleanly.
#
# Entries below are libssl1.1 backports where Canonical fixed the CVE in
# the focal-security pool (current pin: 1.1.1f-1ubuntu2.24) but Trivy's
# database matches the upstream OpenSSL version string and doesn't see
# the backport.  Each Ubuntu Security tracker entry confirms the focal
# patch level we ship has the fix.
#
# When the libssl1.1 pin is eventually bumped (Dependabot or a manual
# version refresh), re-validate each entry against Ubuntu's tracker.
# Remove anything no longer applicable.

# https://ubuntu.com/security/CVE-2021-3449
# fixed in focal 1.1.1f-1ubuntu2.4 (we ship 1.1.1f-1ubuntu2.24)
CVE-2021-3449

# https://ubuntu.com/security/CVE-2021-3711
# fixed in focal 1.1.1f-1ubuntu2.8
CVE-2021-3711

